“System Display and Search Facility” is historically accurate, but it is not a particularly good description of what SDSF means to a user today. Your instinct is right.

The key thing you may be underweighting is that SDSF is broader than simply a job queue viewer. A better conceptual description is:

	SDSF is the z/OS operational console for monitoring and controlling jobs, spool output, and system activity.

	Why “Display and Search Facility” feels wrong	

	The name reflects SDSF's origins as a facility for displaying and searching system/spool information. Its role subsequently became much broader.

	From an ISPF user's perspective, the important concepts are more like

                       SDSF
						|
			+-----------+-----------+
			|           |           |
		  JOBS        OUTPUT      SYSTEM
			|           |           |
		 Running      SYSOUT      Address spaces
		 Waiting      JES spool   System log
		 Held         Job logs    Processes
		 Completed
			|
		 CONTROL
			|
		 Hold / Release
		 Cancel / Purge
		 etc.

	So “Job Queue Monitor” is closer, but still a little too narrow because SDSF exposes system-level operational information in addition to JES job queues.

	What I would call it

	For something like your FileForgeWorkbench, where you're reproducing the concept rather than IBM's historical terminology, I'd seriously consider:

	Job & System Monitor

	That's probably my favourite.

	It immediately tells the user what is behind the menu item:

	Job & System Monitor

	with functions beneath it such as:

	Jobs
	├── Active
	├── Waiting
	├── Held
	├── Completed
	└── All Jobs
	
	Output
	├── Job Output
	├── Held Output
	└── Spool
	
	System
	├── Processes / Address Spaces
	├── System Log
	└── Resources

	Other reasonable names would be:

	Name	My viewJob Monitor	Very clear, but slightly too narrow
	Job & System Monitor	Best general name
	Job Operations	Good if FFWB emphasizes control
	Job Management	Clear, but understates system monitoring
	Workload Monitor	Sounds more like performance/workload management
	Operations Console	Excellent broader abstraction
	Batch Operations	Excellent if you deliberately limit it to batch/JES-like functionality
	Job & Spool Manager	Very precise for JES-oriented functionality
	For FFWB specifically

	I'd separate the IBM compatibility terminology from the conceptual subsystem name.

	For example:

	Operations
	│
	├── Job Monitor
	├── Spool
	├── System Log
	└── System Status

	And internally you could regard the whole subsystem as:

	FFWB Operations Console

	Then an SDSF-compatible interface becomes one UI over that operations subsystem, rather than designing FFWB's architecture around something called “SDSF”.

	That distinction could actually be quite important for FFWB. JES is the underlying job/spool machinery; SDSF is principally an operations interface onto that world. So if you're modelling the architecture, I wouldn't make SDSF itself the job-management engine. I'd make it a front-end/application over your job, spool and system-monitoring services.

	In your FFWB terminology, Operations Console for the subsystem and Job Monitor for the main user-facing panel would be my choice.